                            <p class="heading__desc" style="color: black;">
                                <b style="color: #213360;font-size: 25px;">Introduction</b><br>Keloid scars are raised,
                                thickened patches of skin that develop as an overreaction to a wound, acne, surgery, or
                                injury. While they are harmless, keloids can be physically uncomfortable and
                                aesthetically concerning. With the arrival of summer, managing keloid scars becomes even
                                more critical. The increased heat, humidity, and sun exposure can aggravate these scars,
                                causing itching, swelling, and pigmentation. In this blog, we’ll explore how to care for
                                keloid scars in summer and prevent them from worsening.
                                <br>
                                <br>



                                <b style=" font-size: 25px;color: #213360;">Why Summer Can Worsen Keloids


                                </b><br>
                                During summer, higher temperatures and sun exposure can irritate the skin, leading to
                                inflammation and discoloration of keloid scars. UV rays stimulate melanin production,
                                causing keloids to darken and become more prominent. Additionally, increased sweating
                                can trap bacteria around the scar area, raising the risk of infection or irritation.
                                Wearing tight clothing in hot weather can also cause friction, worsening the appearance
                                and discomfort of keloids.

                                <br>



                                <br>


                                <b style=" font-size: 25px;color: #213360;">How to Manage Keloid Scars in Summer


                                </b><br>
                                Here are proven ways to manage and protect your keloids during the summer season:

                                <br>
                                <b style=" font-size: 20px;color: #213360;">1. Use Broad-Spectrum Sunscreen
                                </b><br>
                                Sun exposure can darken keloid scars. Applying a broad-spectrum sunscreen with SPF 30 or
                                higher helps shield the scar tissue from harmful UV rays. Reapply every two hours,
                                especially if you're outdoors or sweating.<br>

                                <b style=" font-size: 20px;color: #213360;">2. Keep the Area Moisturized
                                </b><br>
                                Hydration is key. Apply a gentle, fragrance-free moisturizer to your keloid to keep the
                                skin soft and prevent it from drying out or cracking. Silicone-based gels and sheets are
                                especially effective in softening the scar tissue and reducing thickness.<br>

                                <b style=" font-size: 20px;color: #213360;">3. Avoid Tight or Irritating Clothing

                                </b><br>



                                Clothes that rub against keloids (especially on shoulders, chest, or back) can cause
                                inflammation. Opt for loose, breathable fabrics like cotton to avoid friction and allow
                                your skin to breathe.

                                <br>

                                <b style=" font-size: 20px;color: #213360;">4. Don’t Scratch or Pick

                                </b><br>
                                Sweat can make keloids itchy, but scratching them can lead to infections or worsen their
                                size. Apply a cooling gel (like aloe vera) to soothe irritation instead.<br>








                                <b style="color: #213360;font-size: 25px;">Treatment Options for Keloids



                                </b><br>
                                If your keloids are becoming more uncomfortable or prominent, consider professional
                                treatments:<br>


                                <b>Laser Therapy:

                                </b><br>
                                Helps reduce the thickness and pigmentation of keloids with minimal downtime.<br>

                               
                                <b>Steroid Injections: </b><br>
                                Corticosteroid injections reduce inflammation and flatten the scar.<br>

                               
                                <b>Cryotherapy</b><br>
                                Freezes the scar tissue, reducing its size.<br>
                                <b>Surgical Removal: </b><br>
                                For severe cases, surgical excision followed by preventive care is an option.<br>
                                At SkinCity, we offer customized keloid treatment plans based on your skin type and
                                severity of the scar. Our experts use the latest technology to ensure safe and lasting
                                results.<br>

                                <b style="color: #213360;font-size: 25px;">Conclusion:</b><br>
                                Summer can pose challenges for individuals with keloid scars, but with the right care
                                and preventive steps, you can protect your skin and prevent further irritation. Don’t
                                let the heat hold you back—stay consistent with your skincare routine and consult a
                                dermatologist for advanced treatments.

                                Book your consultation at SkinCity today and let us help you manage your keloids the
                                right way this summer!































































